# 01 Partial matching: PCRE2_PARTIAL_SOFT / PCRE2_PARTIAL_HARD
# https://www.pcre.org/current/doc/html/pcre2partial.html
#
# Not exposed by PHP.
#
# What it is: a third match outcome. When the end of the subject arrives
# mid-attempt and more input could still complete the match, pcre2_match()
# returns PCRE2_ERROR_PARTIAL instead of "no match".
# Soft (\=ps) prefers a complete match. Partial is reported only when no
# complete match exists in the input.
# Hard (\=ph) reports partial the moment input runs out mid-attempt. The
# subject is treated as a stream prefix, so even $ refuses to commit.
#
# What it enables: type-ahead validation, where a prefix answers "could
# this still become valid?". Also scanning input that arrives in chunks.

# Soft vs hard on the same subject

# Expect: 0: dog
# Soft prefers the complete match. "dog" matches and (sbody)? stays
# unmatched.
/dog(sbody)?/
    dogsb\=ps

# Expect: Partial match: dogsb
# Hard prefers the partial. The attempt at "dogsbody" ran out of input and
# more input may still arrive.
/dog(sbody)?/
    dogsb\=ph

# Type-ahead validation of a date: the use case PHP cannot express

# Expect: Partial match: 2026
# A valid prefix answers "keep typing".
/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
    2026\=ph

# Expect: Partial match: 2026-
/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
    2026-\=ph

# Expect: Partial match: 2026-09-2
/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
    2026-09-2\=ph

# Expect: Partial match: 2026-09-25
# Input that LOOKS complete stays partial under hard semantics. $ cannot
# commit while more input may come.
/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
    2026-09-25\=ph

# Expect: 0: 2026-09-25
# The closed-stream check: same subject with \=ph dropped, so $ commits.
/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
    2026-09-25

# Expect: No match
# A wrong character can never be repaired by more input, even under \=ph.
/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
    2026/09\=ph
